<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karenni people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Red Karen, Kayah people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karenni, also called the Red Karen or Kayah, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-people">Karen people</a> of the hills of eastern Myanmar, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayah-state">Kayah State</a> and across the border in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. In the 19th century their chiefs ruled several small states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> recognised as independent in 1875. Many Karenni have resisted Burmese central rule since independence in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karenni-people.jpg" alt="Karenni people" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ericwinny · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kayah1120.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
